Pygmalion and the Statue
[size=3]
Pygmalion and the Statue
[size=2]
{gray}Metamorphoses
[size=2]
Ovid


Pygmalion lived on the island of Cyprus, and was so digusted upon seeing the {mlcallback=!1}sins of the island's prostitutes{!c} that he swore to remain single and celibate. In the meantime, from ivory {mlcallback=!3}he sculpted{!c} a pure maiden beyond compare, and quickly fell in love with it. The statue was so skillfully sculpted that it seemed not a sculpture, but living woman.

So enamored with the statue Pygmalion would occasionally touch her, hoping that he would feel flesh and not ivory. He would kiss her, and believed {mlcallback=!4}his kisses were returned.{!c} He would speak to her, hold her as if she were soft enough to bruise. He began to present her gifts of jewelery, flowers, and fine clothing. He laid her upon his bed, {mlcallback=!5}comfortably laid among many pillows{!c}, and slept beside her as though she was his wife.

When the festival day for Aphrodite came, Pygmalion gave his offerings and prayed at her temple. He wished only for a wife that looked like his statue, as he was too embarassed to wish for a wife that was exactly the statue. Aphrodite herself was present and, being moved, granted his true desire.

Upon returning to his home Pygmalion kisses his statue and felt warmth. He embraced her and felt the ivory soften. {mlcallback=!6}Shocked and delighted,{!c} yet fearing it not true, he reached out to her again and discovered she was truly alive. Pygmalion thanked Aphrodite many times before one last kiss. The maiden blushed and raised her eyes, gazing for the {mlcallback=!8}first time{!c} at both her lover and the light of the sky.

{mlcallback=!7}The two married{!c1}, which Aphrodite herself was present for, and had a child named Paphos, after whom the Cypriot city is named.

[note=1]
In Ovid's telling, these women had previously been cursed by Aphrodite to be cold and shameless because they dared to defy her. Aren't those who fight back against those above them admirable rather than pitiable?
[note=3]
I know this says that he's a sculptor but in some accounts Pygmalion doesn't create the statue. Rather than falling in love with his own art, the important part is that he falls in love with a figure and begins to shape it himself.
[note=4]
Weren't they?
[note=5]
Is affection without the expectation of reciprocity anything but true love?
[note=6]
Given his surprise Pygmalion doesn't seem at all deluded as to what the statue is. Yet, in spite of that, he still loves her unconditionally.
[note=7]
Isn't it interesting how there's a happy ending given how Greek myths normally end? Modern retellings often attempt to hack in some sort of ironic consequence, which I think is very sad. It's like people miss the beauty of devotion and unrequited love.
[note=8]
I wonder what it would be like to gaze upon your lover like that after being ripped from a pure world of ivory.The Joy of Fish
[size=3]
The Joy of Fish
[size=2]
{gray}Zhuangzi

Zhuangzi and Huizi wandered along a bridge over the Hao River.

Zhuangzi said, "See how these fish drift about. That is the {mlcallback=!2}joy of fish.{!c} "

Huizi replied, "You are not a fish. From where do you know that they are joyful?"

Zhuangzi said, "You are not me. From where do you know I don't know?"

Huizi replied, "{mlcallback=!3}I am not you, so I don't know what you know.{!c} So, since you are not a fish, {mlcallback=!4}you can't know{!c} the joy the fish know!

Zhuangzi said, "Let's start again. When you asked, "From where do you know", {mlcallback=!1}you had already assumed that I knew.{!c} I knew it {mlcallback=!5}from us wandering{!c} to this bridge over the river Hao!

[note=1]
We're built upon so many assumptions that some people forget about them in their attempts at logic. To have a conversation or connection with someone is to accept them as an independent person - one that can reciprocate the same feelings we have.
[note=2]
Like Zhuangzi and the fish, I also enjoy spending my time with those close to me :D
[note=3]
I don't understand how you could have a conversation without having some idea of what the other person knows.
[note=4]
How does he know what he can't know? I don't like this guy at all.
[note=5]
It's the instinctive, animalistic knowledge that's the most important. It's what seperates us from the cold machines!Eshu's Hat
[size=3]
Eshu's Hat
[size=2]
{gray}Yoruba Folktale

Eshu, god of mischief, was walking down the road one day, wearing a hat that was white on one side and black on the other. He walked between two friends who were tending to their fields, and soon after he passed the neighbors began to argue about the color of his hat. {mlcallback=!2}To sow further chaos{!c}, the god turned both himself and his hat around and walked between them once again, which made the two even more convinced of their positions.

When the arguing escalated to the point of violence, Eshu approached the men. He showed the men his hat, revealing that it was he who caused their quarrel. He pointed out that while they were friendly neighbors {mlcallback=!1}they failed to take he, Eshu, into account,{!c} and that is what lead to their brutal fight.

[note=2]
You may see this as a malevolent force intentionally dividing people. However, Eshu is a god - a force of nature - so I think it should be taken as two people finding different views of reality, rather than falling for a trick. 
[note=1]
Sometimes, against all evidence, we have to take a leap of faith and accept that an unbelievable reality can exist alongside our own.The Crane Wife
[size=3]
The Crane Wife
[size=2]
{gray}Japanese Folktale

A poor man was gathering firewood when he came across an injured crane. The man tended to its wounds, and the crane happily flew away. The next day, a woman appeared at his door and asked to be taken as his wife. He could not believe such a beautiful maiden would want a poor man such as him, and so he agreed.

The woman says that she wishes to use his workshop's loom to create a brocade, and that he must vow not look at her work while she does so. The man agrees and she retreats to the room to begin her work. From the other side of the door the man hears strange sounds as she works and wonders how she could weave without thread. Still, he keeps his promise and does not look. After seven days pass she emerges from the room emaciated yet carrying a beautiful piece of cloth. She tells him to sell it the local lord for a high price, and he does so, making them wealthy.

The lord returns to the man and demands he create another brocade. The man tells his wife of the order, and she is reluctant but unable to refuse. Once again she makes him promise not to look while she works. Again the man hears her working but this time, unable to control his curiosity, decides to peek through the door. Instead of his wife he sees the same crane that he had saved some time ago, {mlcallback=!2}plucking its own feather to use as thread.{!c} The crane, covered in red spots, had painfully plucked nearly all of its feather.

Again, after seven days had passed, the woman emerged looking worse than before but with another beautiful piece of cloth. She explained that she knew he had broken his promise and seen her true form. She had taken the form of a woman {mlcallback=!1}to be by his side and repay his kindness{!c} but, since he knew what she truly was, she could no longer stay with him. She turned back into a crane and flew away, never to be seen by the man again.

[note=1]
There's a few examples of cranes in captivity falling in love with their handlers. Are the cranes wrong to do so? Are they defective? Confused? Why, then, when a dog stays next to their owner's grave, do we call that loyalty?
[note=2]
Of course, for the ones we love, we have to keep painful secrets.Thoth Invents Writing
[size=3]
Thoth Invents Writing
[size=2]
{gray}Phaedrus
[size=2]
Plato

There is an old Egyptian tale of Thoth, the inventor of writing, showing his invention to the god Thamus, who told him that he would only spoil men's memories and take away their understandings. From this tale, of which young Athens will probably make fun, may be gathered the lesson that writing is inferior to speech. For it is like a picture, which can give no answer to a question, and has only a deceitful likeness of a living creature. It has no power of adaptation, but uses the same words for all. It is not a legitimate son of knowledge, but a bastard, and when an attack is made upon this bastard neither parent nor anyone else is there to defend it. The husbandman will not seriously incline to sow his seed in such a hot-bed or garden of Adonis; he will rather sow in the natural soil of the human soul which has depth of earth; and he will anticipate the inner growth of the mind, by writing only, if at all, as a remedy against old age. The natural process will be far nobler, and will bring forth fruit in the minds of others as well as in his own. 
Either/Or
[size=3]
Either/Or
[size=2]
Soren Kierkegaard

Marry, and you will regret it; don't marry, you will also regret it; marry or don't marry, you will regret it either way. Laugh at the world's foolishness, you will regret it; weep over it, you will regret that too; laugh at the world's foolishness or weep over it, you will regret both. Believe a woman, you will regret it; believe her not, you will also regret it. Hang yourself, you will regret it; do not hang yourself, and you will regret that too; hang yourself or don't hang yourself, you'll regret it either way; whether you hang yourself or do not hang yourself, you will regret both. This, gentlemen, is the essence of all philosophy.
Interview\\Introductions
[size=2]
An Interview With Alexei  
[size=2]
Part 1

[I]
Some consider him the most important mathematician of the 21st century, others the most controversial. A pioneer in the emerging field of perceptual computation, he is known for his seminal paper on multi-basis computation. Answering one of the most important problems in computer science by proving that P does not equal NP, it has also led to billions spent in attempts to see it done in practice. Joining us today is the well known but ever elusive Alexei Zhakarov. Alexei, thank you for taking the time to join me.
[A]
Of course.
[I]
For the sake of full disclosure, I should say that Alexei and I have known each other for a while - he's my uncle. Would it be accurate to say that you would have turned down this interview otherwise?
[A]
That is correct.
[I]
My audience is lucky, then, that your convictions aren't immune to my nagging. Despite refusing interviews, you've made many public statements. Do you have anything you'd like to say before we begin?
[A]
I do not.
[I]
A man of few words. Let's hope that the following questions will get more out of you.Interview\\Dialectic
[size=2]
An Interview With Alexei  
[size=2]
Part 2

[I]
Your work allows for much faster computation. Can you explain in this in simple terms that our audience can understand?
[A]
Some problems require trying every possibility to get the right answer.
[I]
Like trying to guess someone's password.
[A]
Yes, that can be our example here; now imagine if you could try every possible password at the same time and also discard every attempt that's wrong. You only end up with in the version where the password was correct.
[I]
I see.
[A]
Quantum computation by itself allows for something similar to trying all possibilities, but when you get to the end there's only a chance you end up with the right answer. You need to add something that always guarantees the correct answer.
[I]
So that's where the two concepts of perceptual computation and quantum computation come together.
[A]
Yes. You can consider one doing every possibility, while the other evaluates each of those possibilities. That's why it becomes so powerful when you have both.
Interview\\Mathematics
[size=2]
An Interview With Alexei  
[size=2]
Part 3

[I]
Your work in theory of computation, where you prove the inequality of P vs NP in classical computation, earned you the Millennium Prize. You, much like the previous recipient, didn't accept the award. Can you elaborate on why?
[A]
I cannot speak to the motivation of others. For myself, I refused the prize because I disagree with the current state of the academic community as a whole.
[I]
Still, I think most would take the million dollar reward even if they disagreed with it.
[A]
If my goal was to make money I wouldn't be doing research.
[I]
What is your goal, then?
[A]
I enjoy solving hard problems. It would be better if more academic research was done for that rather than commercial reasons. As it is it falls to people like me to pick up the slack.
[I]
You don't believe that researchers within academia have similar goals?
[A]
There's many individuals who do, but on the whole the academic community selects for those who can publish as opposed to those who do good research. Richard Feynman only published 37 papers, which would hardly be acceptable now. Peter Higgs admitted he wouldn't be able to get an academic job today.
[I]
There's still the private sector.
[A]
The private sector is even worse for falling victim to bad metrics. There's hardly a place for the clever layabout types, which is why I try to find work for them.
[I]
You are rather famous for working with teams that are seen as, um, outside the mainstream.Interview\\Discovery
[size=2]
An Interview With Alexei  
[size=2]
Part 4

[I]
There has been a lot of discussion about the nature of this "perceptual computation". It has implications in subjects stretching from physics to neuroscience to epistemology.
[A]
My work was in the field of computer science. I would be speaking beyond my expertise if I were to speculate on anything outside of that.
[I]
But your research still introduces the idea that our brains run off a unique model of computation, doesn't it?
[A]
As I said, I can't answer that without speculating. The work I did was to rectify the discrepency found in the performance of brain-computer interfaces, and my system of multi-basis computation provided an answer.
[I]
And how did you come up with that theory?
[A]
This breakthrough, like so many others, came from an accident. The more complex these brain-computer interfaces became, the more researchers noticed discrepency in the computation. The people working on the technology didn't appreciate what they had managed to do.
[I]
Do you think computers built from your design will lead to their own set of new breakthroughs?
[A]
History shows that it's a certainty.Interview\\Missing Pieces
[size=2]
An Interview With Alexei  
[size=2]
Part 5

[I]
A computer built with your principles allows certain algorithms to be done that would otherwise take an impossibly long time. The ability to crack all current encryption, for example, would render all systems insecure - famously, the price of cryptocurrency plummeted following your publication. Why has nobody been able to build one yet?
[A]
It is simple enough to build something small that demonstrates the principles, but any attempts to scale up will result in problems with decoherence. Early attempts at building quantum computers had the same issue. While work on that has made great strides the issue becomes exponentially more difficult when you introduce another computational dimension.
[I]
Many skeptics say it's not just difficult, but impossible. Billions of dollars have already been spent by governments, investors and corporations yet they've seen little progress.
[A]
I don't believe that a large organization will be able to solve the issues. Short term thinking is incompatible with the innovation necessary to tackle such a difficult problem.
[I]
There's a popular online conspiracy that you have solved this problem and are keeping it a secret.
[A]
I have been the subject of many rumours.
[I]
So you would deny this one, of course?Interview\\Security
[size=2]
An Interview With Alexei  
[size=2]
Part 6

[I]
There's been a lot of debate over how these new computers could bypass modern security measures and disrupt global systems. Do you have any concerns over how this technology may be used by bad actors?
[A]
Technology will progress regardless. The only proper response is to update existing systems' security.
[I]
There's still many technologies that have been rendered obsolete. Many existing cryptocurrencies have seen their price crash due to concerns that any wallet could easily be hacked with this technology.
[A]
Any team that manages to build a working prototype should also have enough sense to tread lightly.
[I]
What are the odds that someone has already built one that works and is using it?
[A]
If one was being used for anything significant it would quickly become obvious.
[I]
So if someone does have one they're only using it to recover their password without calling support.
[A]
I doubt anyone would actually do that, but I can't deny the possibility.rooms = [
